"""Fixed original MenuNori guide text; run to regenerate public HTML."""
guides=[
('korean-kiosk','How to Use a Korean Restaurant Kiosk','Learn how to use a Korean restaurant kiosk: choose dine-in or takeaway, read menu buttons, set options and check your order. Includes English translations.', '''<p class="lead">To order at a Korean restaurant kiosk, choose dine-in or takeaway, select a dish, set the quantity and options, then check the basket before payment. You don’t need to read every word: start with the labels that change your order. Screen layouts and the order of these steps vary.</p><h2>1. Look for a language choice</h2><p>If you see English or a language symbol, use it. An English screen can still show Korean dish names or abbreviations. If there is no language option, a translation app can help you read the labels. MenuNori prepares you to understand the flow; it does not replace translation or staff assistance at the restaurant.</p><h2>2. Choose where you will eat</h2><p>A kiosk may ask you to select 매장 (dine in) or 포장 (takeaway) before showing the menu. Another may ask near checkout. Choose according to what you actually plan to do. Takeaway packaging or prices can differ at individual restaurants; read the current screen rather than assuming the practice total will apply.</p><h2>3. Select a dish, then read its options</h2><p>On our fictional menu, tapping 떡볶이 opens spice and cheese options. The menu price is ₩4,500; adding cheese costs another ₩1,000. That makes one portion ₩5,500. Selecting two portions makes ₩11,000. Real kiosks may use different layouts, required options, or portion rules.</p><p>Distinguish an included ingredient from an extra: 치즈떡볶이 is a named dish with cheese, while 치즈 추가 is an option to add cheese to another dish. Choosing both without reading carefully could add more than you intended.</p><h2>4. Review the basket before paying</h2><p>장바구니 means basket. Check each dish name and the number beside it. 수량 is quantity; 삭제 removes an item; 합계 is total. Do not use the final button as a way to see what happens: on a real kiosk it can move you into payment.</p><table><tr><th>Korean</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr><tr><td>주문</td><td>Order</td></tr><tr><td>수량</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>추가</td><td>Add / extra</td></tr><tr><td>삭제</td><td>Remove</td></tr><tr><td>확인</td><td>Confirm / check</td></tr><tr><td>결제</td><td>Payment</td></tr></table><h2>5. Follow the restaurant’s collection instructions</h2><p>After a real payment, the machine may display an order number or print a receipt. Keep it and follow the restaurant’s instructions. Some restaurants use a pickup counter; some bring food to your table. MenuNori stops at a simulated confirmation and never asks for a card, PIN, or payment details.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>주문하는 것 좀 도와주세요.</strong><small>Please help me place an order.</small></div><h2>Try this before your trip</h2><p>Complete the first mission without opening the hint. Then look at your basket and explain why you chose 포장. You have practiced the decision that matters, even if a real machine puts that button somewhere else.</p><p class="note">For general travel context, the <a href="https://english.visitkorea.or.kr/svc/contents/contentsView.do?menuSn=903&amp;vcontsId=229195" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Korea Tourism Organization’s dining guide</a> discusses self-service and ordering machines. This article’s menu examples are fictional.</p>'''),
('dine-in-takeaway','To Go in Korean: Takeaway vs Dine In (포장 vs 매장)','Learn what 포장 and 매장 mean on a Korean restaurant kiosk, which button is takeaway, and how to ask for your food to go. Try a fictional order.', '''<p class="lead">For “to go” or takeaway on a Korean restaurant screen, look for 포장 (po-jang). For dine-in, look for 매장 (mae-jang). 매장 means the shop or premises; 포장 means packaging. In this ordering context, they tell the restaurant where you plan to eat.</p><h2>The decision comes before the food</h2><p>Imagine you want gimbap for a picnic. You find 김밥 and choose one portion. The food is right, but selecting 매장 tells the restaurant you intend to eat there. Correct the order type before you finish. MenuNori deliberately starts with dine-in selected so that you practice noticing this detail.</p><table><tr><th>Screen word</th><th>Meaning here</th><th>Say it</th></tr><tr><td>매장</td><td>Dine in / for here</td><td>mae-jang</td></tr><tr><td>포장</td><td>Takeaway / to go</td><td>po-jang</td></tr><tr><td>매장 식사</td><td>Eating at the restaurant</td><td>mae-jang sik-sa</td></tr><tr><td>포장 주문</td><td>Takeaway order</td><td>po-jang ju-mun</td></tr></table><h2>What might change after your choice?</h2><p>The restaurant may serve your food on a tray or pack it into containers. Available menu items, packaging charges, and pickup instructions can vary. Our practice shop keeps the same menu and price in both modes so you can learn the words first. It is not a promise about prices or packaging at a real restaurant.</p><h2>Short phrases are enough</h2><div class="phrase"><strong>여기서 먹고 갈게요.</strong><small>I’ll eat here.</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>포장해 주세요.</strong><small>To go, please.</small></div><p>You can name the dish and add the phrase: 김밥 하나 포장해 주세요 means “One gimbap to go, please.” 하나 is one. You do not need a long explanation of your plans. A clear choice and a polite 주세요 are useful starting points.</p><h2>If you selected the wrong button</h2><p>Before checkout, look for an order-type setting or return to the previous screen. If you cannot change it, ask a staff member before paying. After payment, ask whether the restaurant can change the packaging; it may not always be possible.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>포장으로 바꿀 수 있어요?</strong><small>Can I change this to takeaway?</small></div><h2>Practice the same food in both modes</h2><p>In mission 1, choose 포장 and add one gimbap and one portion of dumplings. Read the order-type line in the basket before confirming. For a second attempt, leave 매장 selected and see the feedback. The food total stays the same, but the mission is incomplete because the order type is wrong.</p><p class="note">The general meaning of a word and the label on a particular machine are not the same thing. If a screen is unclear, use translation or ask staff. This rehearsal teaches a common distinction, not a universal kiosk layout.</p>'''),
('read-korean-menu','How to Read a Korean Menu: A Bunsik Guide','Read a Korean restaurant menu with six bunsik dish names in English. Recognize gimbap, tteokbokki, ramyeon, prices, portions and extra options.', '''<p class="lead">To read a Korean menu, separate the dish name, the price and any options. This English guide uses six dishes from a fictional bunsik snack shop. Start with the name of the food you want; you do not need to translate the entire menu.</p><h2>Six names from our practice shop</h2><p>Bunsik (분식) refers to a casual category of food that includes dishes such as gimbap and tteokbokki. You may also see gimbap written as kimbap, and ramyeon as ramyun; use the Korean name to match the menu entry. Our invented restaurant uses six recognizable dish names. The descriptions below explain our practice recipes; actual ingredients and preparation vary.</p><table><tr><th>Korean</th><th>English name</th><th>Our practice dish</th></tr><tr><td>김밥</td><td>Gimbap</td><td>Rice, vegetables, egg and ham in seaweed</td></tr><tr><td>떡볶이</td><td>Tteokbokki</td><td>Rice cakes in spicy sauce</td></tr><tr><td>라면</td><td>Ramyeon</td><td>Instant noodles in spicy broth</td></tr><tr><td>어묵탕</td><td>Fish cake soup</td><td>Fish cakes in savory broth</td></tr><tr><td>군만두</td><td>Fried dumplings</td><td>A portion of crisp dumplings</td></tr><tr><td>치즈떡볶이</td><td>Cheese tteokbokki</td><td>Tteokbokki with cheese included</td></tr></table><h2>Recognize the whole name</h2><p>김밥 is not the same as 참치김밥, which adds tuna to the name. Similarly, 치즈 before 떡볶이 changes the dish you are choosing. Match the complete name rather than stopping at the first familiar part. If a name includes an ingredient you need to avoid, check with staff before ordering.</p><h2>A portion is not always one piece</h2><p>In our menu, 군만두 하나 means one menu portion of dumplings, not one individual dumpling. A real menu may specify pieces, grams, or servings. Look for 개 (pieces or count), 인분 (servings), and 수량 (quantity). The plus button usually changes how many menu units you order; it does not necessarily change the number of pieces inside one portion.</p><h2>Read prices and extras separately</h2><p>₩3,500 is three thousand five hundred Korean won. In our shop, two gimbap cost ₩7,000. An extra is priced separately: 치즈 추가 +₩1,000 adds cheese to one tteokbokki portion. If you order two of that configured item, the extra applies to each portion in this simulation.</p><p>Real screens may combine options differently. Before paying, use the basket total to check that your interpretation matches what the machine calculated.</p><h2>When you cannot read a name</h2><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 뭐예요?</strong><small>What is this?</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 하나 주세요.</strong><small>One of this, please.</small></div><p>These phrases can help when you can point to the item. A food photograph is a clue, not an ingredient list. Do not assume a dish is vegetarian, free of allergens, or non-spicy based on its appearance.</p><h2>Your rehearsal</h2><p>Find 김밥, 어묵탕, and 군만두 without using the English names. Then run mission 3 and increase 라면 to two. Check that your basket has two noodle portions and only one dumpling portion.</p>'''),
('spice-and-extras','Korean Menu Spice Levels and Extra Options','Understand Korean spice-level labels 순한맛 and 보통맛, spot extra options such as cheese, and check your selection. Mild does not mean spice-free.', '''<p class="lead">Choosing the dish is only half the decision. The next screen may ask about spice or extras. Read the option labels before adding the food to your basket.</p><h2>Four useful option words</h2><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use in our shop</th></tr><tr><td>맵기</td><td>Spice level</td><td>Heading above the spice choices</td></tr><tr><td>순한맛</td><td>Mild flavor</td><td>Less spicy than the regular option</td></tr><tr><td>보통맛</td><td>Regular flavor</td><td>Our default spice choice</td></tr><tr><td>추가</td><td>Add / extra</td><td>An optional additional ingredient</td></tr></table><h2>Mild does not mean no heat</h2><p>Our mild tteokbokki and ramyeon still contain some spice. A restaurant’s mild option can also be hotter than you expect. A level name is relative to that restaurant and dish; there is no universal scale that guarantees your comfort.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 매워요?</strong><small>Is this spicy?</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>안 맵게 해 주세요.</strong><small>Please make it not spicy.</small></div><p>A request does not guarantee that the recipe can be changed. Some sauces or packaged broths are already prepared. If staff say it cannot be made less spicy, choose another dish instead of assuming your request removes the heat.</p><h2>An extra and a separate dish are different</h2><p>Mission 2 asks for 떡볶이 with 치즈 추가. Selecting the separate 치즈떡볶이 dish does not satisfy this exercise, even though both totals can be ₩5,500. The mission teaches you to find and use the extra option, rather than selecting a different menu entry.</p><p>In this rehearsal, the basic tteokbokki is ₩4,500 and extra cheese is ₩1,000 per portion. Cheese tteokbokki has cheese included in its listed price. Read the item name and the option line together in the basket.</p><h2>Do not turn spice labels into dietary advice</h2><p>“Not spicy” describes the heat level of our fictional recipe. It says nothing about allergens, meat, fish, or animal ingredients. Our gimbap includes egg and ham; fish cake soup contains fish; dumpling fillings vary in real restaurants. If you have an allergy or dietary requirement, verify the actual ingredients and preparation with staff.</p><h2>Check your options before confirming</h2><p>Open 떡볶이, choose 순한맛, then select 치즈 추가. Add one portion and look for both choices underneath the dish in your basket. If you used the wrong option, remove that configured item and add it again with the right choices. Mission 2 only passes when the mode, dish, quantity, spice, and extra all match.</p><p class="note">MenuNori does not provide ingredient verification or allergy guarantees. Use this game to learn labels, and rely on the real restaurant for information about the food it serves.</p>'''),
('check-your-order','Korean Kiosk Orders: What to Check Before Paying','Check a Korean kiosk order before paying: dish names, quantities, spice, extras, takeaway or dine-in, and totals. Practice finding a basket mistake.', '''<p class="lead">The best time to catch an ordering mistake is before payment. Use five checks: where, what, how many, which options, and how much.</p><h2>Where: read the order type</h2><p>매장 means eating at the restaurant in this context; 포장 means taking the order away. Do not assume the machine remembers your previous visit or the selection you wanted. Read the current setting in the basket.</p><h2>What: match complete dish names</h2><p>떡볶이 and 치즈떡볶이 are different menu entries. An item photograph can look similar even when the name differs. In mission 4, an incorrect tteokbokki is already in the basket. You need to remove it, then add fish cake soup alongside one gimbap. Adding the correct dishes without deleting the wrong one does not finish the mission.</p><h2>How many: check each line</h2><p>수량 means quantity. Two noodle portions and one dumpling portion should appear as 라면 × 2 and 군만두 × 1. A cart badge showing 3 only tells you the overall quantity; it cannot tell you whether the three portions are the right ones.</p><h2>Which options: look underneath the name</h2><p>Check for 순한맛 (mild), 보통맛 (regular), and 치즈 추가 (extra cheese) where relevant. If the option you want is missing, go back before paying. In MenuNori you can remove a line and add the dish again. A real kiosk may also offer a direct edit button.</p><h2>How much: do one simple calculation</h2><table><tr><th>Practice order</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>1 gimbap + 1 dumpling portion</td><td>₩3,500 + ₩3,500</td><td>₩7,000</td></tr><tr><td>1 tteokbokki + cheese</td><td>₩4,500 + ₩1,000</td><td>₩5,500</td></tr><tr><td>2 ramyeon + 1 dumpling portion</td><td>2 × ₩4,000 + ₩3,500</td><td>₩11,500</td></tr></table><p>These are invented prices. At an actual restaurant, use its current menu, option prices, and displayed final total. If something is unclear, ask before confirming a payment.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>주문 확인해 주세요.</strong><small>Please check my order.</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 하나 빼 주세요.</strong><small>Please remove one of this.</small></div><h2>Do a deliberate mistake rehearsal</h2><p>Try mission 1 with the right food but the wrong order type. The result explains the mismatch and lets you keep editing. Then correct only the mode and submit again. This is a useful habit: fix the specific mistake rather than restarting a whole order unnecessarily.</p><p>For your final rehearsal, complete mission 5 without hints. Read each line, confirm the total is under ₩9,000, and then place the practice order. You have practiced a repeatable check, not memorized a particular restaurant’s screen.</p><p class="note">Nothing is purchased or sent to a restaurant here. The confirmation screen is a simulation, and no payment information is collected.</p>''')]
from site_pages import build_pages
build_pages(guides)
